Circadian Rhythm Misalignment
Integrative & Functional medicine
Delayed sleep onset, Extreme difficulty waking in the morning, Debilitating daytime somnolence, Late-night energy surge

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IFM-007
Reason for Visit: Extreme daytime somnolence leading to an acute near-miss domestic accident (falling asleep while cooking), requiring immediate assessment to rule out narcolepsy or acute metabolic encephalopathy.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated profound, uncontrollable hypersomnolence resulting in a dangerous domestic near-miss, alongside severe cognitive fog and irritability. Urgent on-site physical and neurological assessment was strictly required to exclude acute hypercapnia, severe obstructive sleep apnea complications, or occult substance ingestion, preventing fatal injury or severe physiological decompensation prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I nearly burned the house down because I physically couldn't keep my eyes open, but I know tonight I'll be staring at the ceiling until 4 AM."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports an evolving 3-month history of a severely inverted sleep-wake cycle, culminating today in extreme, debilitating daytime somnolence. The patient experiences significant difficulty initiating sleep at a conventional bedtime, frequently lying awake for hours, followed by extreme, profound difficulty waking up in the early morning for standard obligations. This afternoon, the patient fell asleep mid-task, prompting the urgent call. The patient reports a natural distinct surge in energy and heightened mental alertness specifically occurring late at night. Additional symptoms include severe brain fog, poor daytime concentration, noticeable mood lability with intense irritability, and digestive disturbances due to misaligned meal timings. The patient admits to frequent reliance on heavy caffeine consumption to function during standard daytime hours.
Functional Status: Currently unsafe to operate machinery, cook, or drive due to severe somnolence.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; acute risk of domestic fire or injury due to uncontrollable microsleeps.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/85 | HR: 90 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 14 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Patient appears disheveled, frequently yawning, with heavily injected conjunctivae and a blunted affect.
Physical Exam: Neurological exam reveals normal cranial nerves, intact motor and sensory pathways, and normal deep tendon reflexes, ruling out focal central lesions. Mental status exam shows significant daytime sluggishness, delayed processing speed, but intact orientation. Airway assessment reveals a Mallampati class II airway without gross tonsillar hypertrophy or obvious morphologic features of severe obstructive sleep apnea. Cardiovascular and respiratory exams are unremarkable. The clinical picture perfectly aligns with extreme difficulty waking in the morning, debilitating daytime somnolence, and late-night energy surges. Confirmation necessitates a 14-day actigraphy log and an abnormal dim light melatonin onset (DLMO) profile.
Environmental Observations: Blinds are drawn tightly shut in the mid-afternoon. Multiple empty energy drink cans observed in the kitchen.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient not taking prescription medications. Heavy consumption of caffeine; no other substances reported.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Circadian Rhythm Misalignment - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Hypercapnic Encephalopathy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal respiratory rate, excellent room air oxygen saturation, and absence of bounding pulses or asterixis.

Narcolepsy (Type 1) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of cataplexy (sudden loss of muscle tone triggered by emotion) or sleep paralysis, and specific presence of a rigidly delayed sleep phase.

Severe Obstructive Sleep Apnea (OSA) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Lack of witnessed apneas, choking episodes, or significant nocturnal snoring, though formal polysomnography is required to absolutely exclude.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, uncontrollable daytime hypersomnolence that directly precipitated a severe domestic safety incident. Telephone triage appropriately identified the urgent need to evaluate for acute metabolic encephalopathy, severe hypoxemia, or central neurological pathology. Immediate on-site clinical assessment was strictly necessary to assess baseline conscious state, perform a focused respiratory and neurological exam, and evaluate the home environment for immediate life threats. The normal neurological findings and stable vital signs excluded acute metabolic or hypoxic emergencies. However, the severity of the somnolence and the immediate risk of accidental injury dictated that delaying assessment until the next in-hours period posed an unacceptable risk to the patient's life and property. This urgent intervention satisfies MBS Items 585-600 criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Instructed patient to immediately unplug all stove/oven appliances and avoid driving. Recommend a detailed 14-day sleep diary and routine GP follow-up for consideration of low-dose exogenous melatonin and bright light therapy.
Positive Behavioral Change: To force phase-shifting of the circadian rhythm, initiate strict photic regulation. Action: Mandate 30 minutes of direct outdoor sunlight exposure immediately upon waking, regardless of the time. Consequence: Intense early light completely suppresses residual melatonin production, directly rewarding the patient with improved morning alertness and an earlier sleep onset that night. Action: Impose a strict zero-caffeine policy after 1200 hours. Consequence: Eliminating afternoon adenosine blockade prevents the late-night energy surge, allowing physiological sleep drive to accumulate safely.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated currently.
DME/Safety (Optional): Light therapy box (10,000 lux) recommended for morning use.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 1 week for actigraphy referral and formal phase-shifting protocol.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department or call 000 if experiencing sudden unarousable unresponsiveness, severe confusion, sudden weakness, or difficulty breathing.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending investigations; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Shifting a rigid circadian rhythm takes weeks of strict behavioral compliance. Short-term fatigue will worsen before it improves as the caffeine is withdrawn.
